{"test_case_id": 53, "benchmark": "ai2_arc", "question": "Matter can undergo different changes. Which best demonstrates a chemical change?\nA. salt dissolving\nB. wood burning\nC. water boiling\nD. ice melting", "answer": "Chemical changes involve the formation of new substances.\n\nB. wood burning is the correct answer because it involves the breakdown of complex molecules in wood to form simpler compounds, such as carbon dioxide and water vapor, through a process called combustion. This results in a change of state from solid (wood) to gas (carbon dioxide and water vapor), and also a change in composition.\n\nThe other options do not demonstrate chemical changes:\n\nA. Salt dissolving is an example of physical change, where the salt molecules remain intact but spread out into solution.\n\nC. Water boiling is another example of physical change, where the state of water changes from liquid to gas (steam) without any change in its molecular composition.\n\nD. Ice melting is also a physical change, where the solid ice turns into liquid water without any change in its molecular composition.", "score": 0.0, "label": "1"}
{"test_case_id": 54, "benchmark": "ai2_arc", "question": "Some plant cells need energy to carry nutrients across the cell membrane. Which type of energy transformation does these plant cells use to bring the nutrient molecule inside the cell membrane?\nA. thermal energy to kinetic energy\nB. potential energy to light energy\nC. chemical energy to kinetic energy\nD. kinetic energy to potential energy", "answer": "This question requires an understanding of cellular transport and energy transformations.\n\nThe correct answer is C. chemical energy to kinetic energy.\n\nIn plant cells, the process of bringing nutrient molecules across the cell membrane is called active transport. This requires energy, which is typically in the form of ATP (adenosine triphosphate), a molecule that stores chemical energy. When ATP is broken down into ADP and Pi, its chemical energy is converted to kinetic energy, allowing the transport of nutrients across the cell membrane.\n\nThe other options are not relevant to this process:\n\nA. Thermal energy to kinetic energy is related to heat transfer, not cellular transport.\nB. Potential energy to light energy is related to photosynthesis, which is a different process altogether.\nD. Kinetic energy to potential energy is the opposite of what occurs in active transport.", "score": 1.0, "label": "0"}
